        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.335,
            0.881,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": "10. Consider 4 points A, B, C, D in the plane with no three collinear. Answer the following questions. Some answers may require you to consider different cases, depending on how the points are positioned in the plane."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.414,
            0.881,
            0.453
          ],
          "content": "(i) How many different quadrilaterals do they form if the quadrilateral is allowed to be self-intersecting or non-convex?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.19,
            0.453,
            0.881,
            0.494
          ],
          "content": "(ii) How many of these quadrilaterals are self-intersecting? How many are convex?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.502,
            0.881,
            0.564
          ],
          "content": "11. Suppose P is a point on side AB of ∆ABC and the line through P parallel to BC meets AC in point Q. For parts (i) to (iii), assume $\\mathrm{AP} = 1, \\mathrm{AQ} = \\sqrt{5}$ , and see Fig. 12.39."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.572,
            0.863,
            0.696
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.451,
                0.703,
                0.549,
                0.721
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 12.39"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_27_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.198,
            0.73,
            0.705,
            0.751
          ],
          "content": "(i) If PB = 2 find QC. (**Hint:** See the dotted line)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.755,
            0.713,
            0.78
          ],
          "content": "\\*(ii) $\\mathrm { I f }   \\mathrm { P } \\bar { \\mathrm { B } } = \\frac { 1 } { 3 }$ find QC. (**Hint:** See the dotted lines)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.173,
            0.785,
            0.424,
            0.811
          ],
          "content": "\\*(iii) If $\\mathrm { P B } = { \\frac { 2 } { 3 } }$ find QC."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.239,
            0.812,
            0.659,
            0.833
          ],
          "content": "(**Hint:** Divide both PB and AP suitably.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.837,
            0.88,
            0.918
          ],
          "content": "\\*(iv) Show that if $\\frac { \\mathrm { A P } } { \\mathrm { P B } }$ is a rational number, then $\\frac{\\mathrm{AP}}{\\mathrm{PB}} = \\frac{\\mathrm{AQ}}{\\mathrm{QC}}$ . In Grade 10, you will prove this equality without assuming $\\frac { \\mathrm { A P } } { \\mathrm { P B } }$ is rational. That will require a new idea."
        }
      ]
    }
